---
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Affiliate Disclosure"
  description="Whether this site carries affiliate links, what a commission would and would not change about the conclusions, and the rules it holds itself to."
>
  <h1>Affiliate Disclosure</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Short version: <strong>there are no affiliate links on this site today.</strong> If that changes, a link that pays me will be disclosed at the top of the post containing it, and <strong>your price will not change</strong>. Commissions will not decide what I recommend. The rest of this page is the policy that applies from the moment the first one appears.
  </p>

  <h2>What an affiliate link is</h2>
  <p>
    An affiliate link is an ordinary link carrying an identifier. When you click it, the merchant can tell the visit came from this site. If you go on to buy or subscribe, they pay me a share of the sale as commission.
  </p>
  <p>
    That money comes out of the merchant's marketing budget. <strong>It is not added to your price.</strong> What you pay going through a link here is the same as what you'd pay searching for the merchant directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Programs this site participates in</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>None, as of October 2026.</strong> No post currently published here
    contains an affiliate link, and no commission has been earned from anything
    on this site.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page exists ahead of that because the policy should be written before
    there is money attached to it, not after. When programs are joined they will
    be listed here by name — naming them is more honest than describing them in
    general terms, and most affiliate programs require it in their terms anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    Until that list has entries, treat every recommendation on this site as
    unpaid.
  </p>
  <p class="meta">
    <!-- TODO: once the accounts are live, list each program by name here
         (e.g. Amazon Associates, PartnerBoost, Impact.com, plus specific SaaS
         programs). Naming them is more credible than a vague description, and
         most affiliate programs require it in their terms of service. -->
  </p>

  <h2>What commissions do and don't affect</h2>
  <p>This is the part of the page that matters, so I'll be specific.</p>

  <h3>What they don't affect</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rankings and conclusions.</strong> Who comes first in a comparison is decided by the test results. I don't move A ahead of B because A pays 30% and B pays nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Criticism.</strong> Products that pay me get their flaws written up exactly like products that don't.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The freedom to say no.</strong> If a product runs an affiliate program and I don't think it's worth buying, I say so and leave the link out.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What they do affect — honestly</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What I choose to test.</strong> I'm more likely to test a product that has an affiliate program, because writing it up might pay. That bias is real, I can't fully remove it, and the least I can do is tell you it exists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Coverage.</strong> Some excellent open-source tools have no affiliate program, and they probably show up here less than they deserve. If you're making a decision, don't treat "what this site covers" as a complete map of what's available.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>My rules</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>No affiliate link for a product I haven't actually used.</li>
    <li>Posts with affiliate links carry the disclosure at the top, not buried at the bottom.</li>
    <li>No paid reviews. If a vendor ever pays for coverage, it gets labelled as sponsored and kept separate.</li>
    <li>No "limited time" or "only a few spots left" urgency tactics.</li>
    <li>When a product gets worse, the conclusion changes — even if it's still paying me. Edits are dated.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Cookies and tracking</h2>
  <p>
    When you click an affiliate link, the merchant or affiliate network may set a cookie in your browser so a later purchase can be attributed to this site. That's how the mechanism works; this site can't switch it off, though you can block third-party cookies in your browser settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    What this site itself collects, and doesn't, is on the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Compliance</h2>
  <p>
    This disclosure is provided in line with the US Federal Trade Commission's Endorsement Guides. If you have a question about any commercial relationship behind something on this site, <a href="/contact/">just ask me</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">Last updated: September 2026</p>
</Base>
